<h2 class="mt-12">Peak season readiness for order tracking</h2>
<ol>
<li>Halt order tracking edits ahead of the peak window.</li>
<li>Order Tracking QA spans purchase, refund, and case paths.</li>
<li>Check order tracking exits filter buyers and open cases.</li>
<li>For order tracking, grow control samples ahead of volume.</li>
<li>Draft order tracking SMS quiet-hour and cap rules in advance.</li>
<li>For order tracking, test tier limits against peak projections.</li>
<li>Order Tracking placement watched every peak day.</li>
<li>Pre-write order tracking rollback moves per flow before launch.</li>
</ol>
<p>One more order tracking operating note: book the first quarterly review before launch, not after the first crisis. Order Tracking teams that record suppression rules, discount caps, and escalation contacts in week one firefight far less later, and new operators inherit a order tracking system instead of folklore.</p>
<p>Finally, review order tracking in writing each quarter: what order tracking work shipped, what was suppressed, what margin the order tracking program kept, which assumptions failed. That order tracking record turns individual judgment into team knowledge and keeps vendor calls evidence-based.</p>
